h him in helping to sort of  put sound sequences together and then  table reads with sound effects and music  and a lot of prep and animatics I mean  even but you know just before we started  shooting baby driver we actually had the  whole film as an animatic and we could  show all the eight roadies head of  departments in Atlanta the entire film  as basically drawn or storyboard which  was a fantastic thing to have for  reference being on set with Edgar I mean  this actually meant not even working in  a truck or a van or something I actually  had a little onset trolley a mag liner  and was literally on the front line of  the crew and there so there's the DOP  there's video assist and there I was so  literally on almost every shot of on  baby driver hook I could tell you where  I was just out of shot and Edgar at that  point said you know we need to make sure  that every shot sort of works for the  Edit and it was a very interesting mix  of production and post-production almost  occurring at the same time actually in  as much as the shot had to work for the  Edit as much as the Edit had to work for  the shot  it was symbiotic one had to basically  satisfy the other we couldn't shoot it  then come back to England to where we  did all the post and suddenly realize we  have a whole raft of issues you know  timings out and there's various  technical issues we wanted to make sure  that we came back from Atlanta with all  the footage if not cut to a fine degree  but at least assembled that we knew we  weren't going to have any any issues and  that was an incredible challenge  probably the hardest job I've ever done  but at the same time probably the most  fun I've ever had to sort of be out  there you know at that process and at  that moment of creation I certainly  haven't done anything quite like that  before for me I realized the number the  number one thing fun enough is not  actually a technical thing it's actually  an emotional thing it's enthusiasm and  it's the on